# Changelog & Project Documentation Discipline

Maintain a clear, current view of what's done, what's changing, and why.

## Required files in the repo

- `CHANGELOG.md` — user-facing changes (follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/))
- `docs/PROGRESS.md` — high-level project status, current milestone, roadmap
- `docs/DECISIONS.md` — Architectural Decision Records (ADRs)

## CHANGELOG.md format

```markdown
# Changelog

## [Unreleased]

### Added
- New features and capabilities

### Changed
- Modifications to existing behavior

### Fixed
- Bug fixes

### Removed
- Removed features (with migration notes)

### Security
- Security-related changes

## [1.2.0] - 2026-05-20
...
```

## When the AI completes a meaningful task, it MUST

1. Update `CHANGELOG.md` under `[Unreleased]` with one-line summary
2. If a milestone or phase is complete, update `docs/PROGRESS.md`
3. If an architectural decision was made (library choice, pattern, structural shift), append an entry to `docs/DECISIONS.md`

## ADR entry template (docs/DECISIONS.md)

```markdown
## ADR-NNN: <Short title>
Date: YYYY-MM-DD
Status: Accepted | Superseded | Deprecated

### Context
What problem are we solving?

### Decision
What did we choose?

### Alternatives considered
What else did we look at?

### Consequences
What changes because of this? Tradeoffs?
```

## PROGRESS.md structure

```markdown
# Project Progress

## Current phase
<e.g. MVP - Authentication module>

## Completed
- [x] Feature A (v0.1.0)
- [x] Feature B (v0.2.0)

## In progress
- [ ] Feature C — 60% — blocker: waiting on design

## Next up
- [ ] Feature D
- [ ] Feature E

## Known risks / debt
- ...
```

## Commit messages (feed into the progress system)

Use Conventional Commits — they auto-categorize for changelog:

- `feat: ...` → Added
- `fix: ...` → Fixed
- `refactor: ...` → Changed (internal)
- `docs: ...` → docs only
- `chore: ...` → tooling
- `BREAKING CHANGE: ...` in body → major version bump
